/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/research/e2e-offering-run-topology.mem:67:* **Compose project names scope resources, while published host ports remain shared.** Docker says distinct Compose project names isolate containers, networks, and related project resources; project name can be overridden by `-p`/`COMPOSE_PROJECT_NAME` (https://docs.docker.com/compose/how-tos/project-name/; https://docs.docker.com/compose/intro/compose-application-model/). Docker documents `HOST_PORT:CONTAINER_PORT` as a host mapping, and Compose can allocate a host port when only a container port is supplied (https://docs.docker.com/reference/compose-file/services/#ports). Here the Compose file pins `name: dreamnet-mesh` and host ports `18090`–`18094`; the scenario script hardcodes container/network names and executes `down -v`. Merely overriding project name would not free the fixed ports, and the script's hardcoded cleanup observations also need to become project-aware. Current safe rule: serialize that runner and any peer using its fixed project/ports/volumes. Future parallelization needs all three: unique project name, dynamic or run-scoped published host ports, and cleanup/query names derived from that same project identity.
