   216	!!! House rulings under the operator's delegation ~ the questions the operator delegated, each with what flips it
   217	The operator delegated these to the house: each stands as a house ruling the operator may overturn, its law lives in the carrier it names, and its build rides the loop rows of #/the-loops.
   218	* ''The ember's key at rest'' (house ruling under the operator's delegation, 2026-10-14). A separate serving key (SK), the fifth custody class `serving`, wrapped by the vessel key and by ember slots, seals the serving rows alone, and the serving tier holds no minting secret: a walker's grant checks under a public half, a shape the hosted road's re-cut lands before the ember's walker arm. An unattended answer (any slot that answers without a person's confirm on the ember) needs a freshness anchor a disk copy cannot carry, a hardware-sealed hearth key whose attestation the ember checks on each ask; without one every ember slot answers only on a person's confirm, threshold two buys a second confirm and never an unattended raise, and after any counted removal a bare deny-only `sk-rotation-owed` mark refuses every ask until SK rolls, a mark a rolled-back disk copy can omit. ''For the operator'': a hearth that holds no hardware anchor raises only on a person's confirm. The reason: a key that opens serving state opens nothing beside it, and a copied disk replays any freshness it carries; the ruling flips on a threat review showing the serving rows hold nothing a thief can use even before the grant re-cut (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/custody#/the-table">>; <<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/ember">>).
   219	* ''The ember's serving gate'' (house ruling under the operator's delegation, 2026-10-14). A third gate tier, the serving route, takes its bundle from SK, and the floor and sovereign tiers stand untouched; a pin moves only by a signed causal event or a verified key-history step, and no quiet period ages a pin out. The reason: a dialer that pinned the sovereign tier never mistakes a serving hearth for an unlocked one. The ruling flips on proof that a gate pin decides no authority anywhere (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/face-proof#/the-gate-exchange">>).
   220	* ''How a raise lapses'' (house ruling under the operator's delegation, 2026-10-14). A raise lives one boot and lapses at the next causal boundary (a lock, a restart, a human unlock, a counted removal), and that boundary IS the expiry the waking floor requires; no timer and no heartbeat ends a standing, and the count spends at the raise ledger's durable commit before SERVING composes, so a crash before the commit spends nothing and serves nothing and a crash after it burns the raise. The reason: quiet never tells a slow device from a gone one; the residue: a rolled-back ledger on a disk copy restores spent raises. The ruling flips on a causal renewal event every reader receives that also survives the ember's loss (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/waking-floor#/the-shape">>).
   221	* ''A removal no reader can keep'' (house ruling under the operator's delegation, 2026-10-14). The based removal refuses by name where no parallel-drafts reader keeps the concurrent words, or every reader failed, and the store authors nothing; the refusal carries a durable conflict handle (the document, the removal's request id, the conflicting change and head identities, never the hand's text), so a hand whose editor went offline finds the same conflict on its return. The reason: a draft the store wrote would choose a bag, a tier and a steward no hand named. The ruling flips on an already-authorized destination for such words that keeps provenance and tier without the store choosing either (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/automerge-doc-store">>).
   222	* ''The outbox at rest'' (house ruling under the operator's delegation, 2026-10-14). Each outbox parcel seals under randomized authenticated encryption with a stable outbox key the vessel key wraps and an unlock hands the daemon island, the way content keys reach it, and rests under a random name with its routing inside the sealed body, so a locked disk names no reader; the `sealed-outbox` row leaves `floor-plain` for a sealed class. Before a parcel's atomic commit nothing stands sendable, after it the parcel re-opens and retries at the next unlock, and after a ship and before the local delete the receiver folds a repeat idempotently. The reason: a reader digest on a stolen disk names a relationship; no flip stands expected, since the ruling closes a residue (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/custody#/the-table">>).
   223	* ''The herm relay leg'' (house ruling under the operator's delegation, 2026-10-14). The ask rides the sealed sibling session under that session's seal with a tagged inner frame, and the delivery receipt stays a bare session note good for the asker alone, bound to a unique request id and the session's causal position, attesting transit and never execution; reach narrows to one PersonaGroup, and the leg lands with `W9`. The reason: the fewest new shapes, and no signing domain minted for a transport fact. The ruling flips on a concrete need for a third party to audit relay delivery after the asker vanishes (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/receipt-ledger#/the-relay-leg">>).
   224	* ''The disk watcher's reach'' (house ruling under the operator's delegation, 2026-10-14). A pending removal settles with no person present only under a standing instruction the operator signs by a human route, naming one bag and a bounded class of removals (a path pattern and a per-wave count ceiling): the watcher executes within it, every settled removal lands in the audit with the instruction it rode, and `lares shore restore` still answers it. An automated seat never signs one, a standoff (disk and records differ) never settles without a person, and a mass vanish past the instruction's ceiling stays pending for a person. The reason: a person who answered in writing for exactly that class has answered, and a count alone proves no intent; the ruling flips on a single incident where an in-scope instruction deleted what a person meant to keep (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/api/disk-projection#/projection-law">>).
